    "q-96924": {
      "dom_id": "step-q96924",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "The minimum time it takes a pizzeria to deliver an order is $15$ minutes, and the maximum time is $27$ minutes. If the delivery time is uniformly distributed, what is the variance in the delivery time?",
      "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the time it takes to deliver a randomly selected order. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[15,27],$ and we wish to compute $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{(b-a)^{2}}{12}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[15,27],$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{(27-15)^{2}}{12}=\\frac{144}{12}=12\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance of the delivery time is $12\\,{\\text{min}}^{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-96924-a",
