    "q-170688": {
      "dom_id": "step-q170688",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "The earliest that a football fan arrives at a match is $15$ minutes before the beginning of the match, and the latest that they arrive is $5$ minutes late. If the arrival times are uniformly distributed, what is the mean arrival time?",
      "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the arrival time of a randomly chosen fan relative to the beginning of the match. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[-15,5],$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[-15,5],$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{-15+5}{2}=-5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean arrival time is $5$ minutes before the beginning of the match.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
